<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n26349" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">leucanthĕmon</span></b>  or <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">-mum</span></b> , i, n., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λευκάνθεμον</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>the name of three plants</i>.
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>The chamomile</i>, called also anthemis,
			leucanthemis, etc., <i><span class="en">Plin. 22, 21, 26, §
			53</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>A plant</i>, called also phalangites and
			leucacantha, <i><span class="en">Plin. 27, 12, 98, §
			124</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>III.</b>
			<i>A plant whose scent resembles that of
			southernwood</i> (abrotonum), <i><span class="en">Plin. 21,
				10, 34, § 60</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
